<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Embedded into the executable, not placed in an .app bundle.

  macOS only grants a window a Retina-resolution drawable when the running
  application declares NSHighResolutionCapable. Without it SDL_WINDOW_HIGH_
  PIXEL_DENSITY does nothing, SDL_GetWindowPixelDensity returns 1.0, and
  everything is rendered at half resolution and upscaled by the compositor --
  which is why the map editor's text looked soft on a Retina panel.

  That key normally lives in Contents/Info.plist of an .app bundle, and a bundle
  is exactly what this port cannot cheaply produce: lsl::GetAppPath returns the
  directory holding the executable, and every asset in the game is resolved
  relative to it. In a bundle the executable sits in Contents/MacOS, so either
  the whole Data tree moves in beside it or GetAppPath has to learn about
  bundles -- and that function is on the path of every texture, sound, mesh and
  level the game loads.

  The linker will place a plist in a __TEXT,__info_plist section instead, and
  the system reads it from there for a bare executable. Same declaration, no
  bundle, no change to how anything is found on disk.

  Kept deliberately minimal: this is not an application bundle and should not
  pretend to be one. A real bundle -- with an icon, a version, a signature --
  is a packaging job for whenever this port ships something.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
